  "verbalization": "Guanidine exhibits a strong binding interaction with Kcnc2, as evidenced by multiple paths in the hetionet dataset, all of which score above 0.67. These interactions are mediated through the delayed rectifier potassium channel activity, which is a known functional pathway associated with Kcnc2. The presence of multiple binding targets (Kcnc3, Kcna7, and Kcnv1) suggests that Guanidine may influence potassium channel activity in different subtypes, potentially through shared mechanisms. The consistent involvement of Kcnc2 in the delayed rectifier potassium channel activity implies that Guanidine could modulate this pathway, possibly by inhibiting or activating the channel. The integrated evidence suggests that Guanidine may exert a pharmacological effect on Kcnc2 through a mechanism involving the delayed rectifier potassium channel, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
